<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Tolentino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1797</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Tolentino was signed on 19 February 1797 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> and the French Republic, represented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>. The Papal States gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and the Comtat Venaissin, ceded the Legations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, left Ancona under French occupation and paid a heavy indemnity, including works of art sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. It began the dismantling of the pope's temporal power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tolentino.jpg" alt="Treaty of Tolentino" width="300" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trait%C3%A9_de_Tolentino_(1797).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
